{-
   What the ownership **analysis** will not accept about elements.

   # resolver: ownership

   A third file, and the reason is the pipeline rather than tidiness: the analysis runs over a
   program the resolver accepted, so it never sees a body with a resolve-time mistake in it. Both of
   these are things the shipping stage accepts, and both are why `Place::Index` is a projection of
   the container's own place here rather than arithmetic on an address.
-}

data Guard {n: I32}

instance Drop(Guard):
    fn drop(->value: Guard) -> {}: {}

fn takeTwo(&a: I32, &b: I32) -> I32 = a + b

fn overlapping() -> I32:
    let &xs = [1, 2] :: [I32 *2]

    {-
       Two exclusive borrows of one element.

       The shipping stage reaches an element through `borrowMut(self.run.items + index)`, so what its
       overlap test sees is a raw address - and rule one there is that two pointer-rooted places
       never conflict. `f(&xs[i], &xs[j])` was therefore accepted with no check of any kind, `xs[0],
       xs[0]` included, and is reachable in safe code as a use-after-free: take two `&String` on one
       element, move out of the first, assign the second, and the assignment's teardown frees a run
       the move now owns.

       Here an element is a projection of `xs`, so `placesOverlap` sees two `Index` steps of one
       parent and `place.h`'s stated rule applies with nothing added.
    -}
    return takeTwo(xs[0], xs[0])

fn moving() -> I32:
    let gs = [Guard {n: 1}, Guard {n: 2}] :: [Guard *2]

    {-
       A move out of an element.

       Which element is gone is not something a move path can hold - the index is a value and the
       length may be a const parameter - so the rest of the run would be left in a state the drop
       placement cannot describe. `exchange` is how such an element is emptied, and it needs no path
       because it puts something back.
    -}
    let ->taken = gs[0]

    return taken.n

fn main() -> I32 = 0
